WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.890
DAVE REISFELD: Hello,
my name is Dave Reisfeld

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.082
and I'm a customer
engineer with Google Cloud.

00:00:10.082 --> 00:00:12.540
In today's session we're going
to talk about modern content

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.130
delivery at scale.

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:18.280
Our agenda for today's session
is a brief introduction

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:21.813
about CDN's in general,
and what is Cloud CDN.

00:00:21.813 --> 00:00:23.980
And then we're going to
talk a little bit about some

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:25.630
of the new features
that have recently

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:28.420
been released on Cloud CDN.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:32.170
We're also going to talk about
some adjunct capabilities

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:34.420
that support Cloud
CDN and really

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:37.030
help provide additional
functionality

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:38.977
for your configurations.

00:00:38.977 --> 00:00:40.810
And lastly, we're going
to talk a little bit

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:43.390
about some upcoming
platform enhancements

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:45.670
that we're making
to both Cloud CDN

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:49.630
that I think you'll be
really interested in seeing.

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:51.130
So we're jumping right in.

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:52.770
What is Cloud CDN?

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:54.690
So before we kind
of go there, let's

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.000
talk a little bit about
what a Content Delivery

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.920
Network is in general.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.830
A Content Delivery Network
is a series of web servers

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:05.790
that are designed to deliver
content close to end users.

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:08.010
Generally speaking,
when customers

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:10.320
look to use a Content
Delivery Network,

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.360
they're really
looking to get access

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.670
to one of five key benefits.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.280
Some performance benefit,
by serving content close

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.510
to end users.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.990
Global reach, being able to
expand beyond their origin

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:25.830
infrastructure and having the
CDN give a point of presence.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:28.110
High availability,
ensuring that there's

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.900
no single point of failure
in the delivery chain.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:35.940
Scalability, both in terms of
vertical and horizontal scaling

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.610
for planned and
unplanned event activity.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.200
But also from a
security standpoint,

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.340
both in terms of secure
content delivery,

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:49.140
but also in terms of denial of
service and security mitigation

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.460
capabilities.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:54.660
So Cloud CDN is Google's
Content Delivery Network

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.660
that has been designed for
accelerating web and video

00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:58.750
content.

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:00.570
The Cloud CDN platform
has been built

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:02.850
to support Google's
exacting needs.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:06.090
What I mean by that is the
Cloud CDN platform that you

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.360
use on Google Cloud
is the same platform

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.020
that Google uses for
delivering search results,

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.030
for delivering Gmail results.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:18.060
So you know that this is a
highly performant and highly

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:21.090
scalable Content Delivery
Network platform.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:23.940
Specifically, as it
relates to Cloud CDN,

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.310
there are various
performance capabilities

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:29.160
that we embed into Cloud CDN.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.040
Things, for example, like
the industry leading time

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.240
to first byte.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.700
These are a lot of
the technical kind

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:40.620
of nuances that we bake
into the Cloud CDN platform

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:44.800
to ensure fast response
time, fast throughput time.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.010
And a lot of that comes
around from Google innovation.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:52.260
Things like Speedy, that
manifest itself as a CGP2.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.150
We also look at
things like QUIC,

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:58.140
and how that is quickly morphing
into the new HTTP/3 protocol.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:01.380
But you also can look at
new standards in pacing,

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.930
for example the
BBR functionality

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:06.090
that you can find
in Cloud CDN, that

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:09.840
was first developed at Google.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:13.410
Global reach, the
Cloud CDN platform

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:17.760
consists of over 140 different
edge locations around the world

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:19.710
in 35 different countries.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:23.910
This ensures that you can have
a point of preference anywhere

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:28.040
around the globe, where
your end users are located.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.470
From a high
availability standpoint,

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:35.460
Google backs up our Cloud
CDN platform with an SLA.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:41.540
A 99.95% availability SLA
to ensure that you always

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:44.240
have your content delivered
and it's always going

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.850
to be available on Cloud CDN.

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:50.480
In terms of scalability,
the Cloud CDN platform

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.410
is a multi terabyte per second
content delivery platform.

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:57.290
This is an active platform,
delivering terabytes

00:03:57.290 --> 00:03:59.970
of data on a daily basis today.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:03.560
This is not a new platform,
but this is simply

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:07.310
the commercial use of Google's
existing content delivery

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:09.050
network platform.

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:11.750
And lastly, from a
security standpoint,

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:15.590
Cloud CDN supports both PCI
and HIPAA based workloads.

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:19.640
You also have the ability
to customize TLS handshakes,

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:21.890
whether it's in terms
of specific ciphers,

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:27.410
or specific TLS protocols
that you want to use.

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:31.580
One of the highlights of
Cloud CDN is the network.

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:35.750
Many of you have been familiar
with the Google Data Centers

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:39.030
within the Cloud program.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:43.850
But added to that are the
edge CDN nodes distributed

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:44.970
around the globe.

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:48.530
Some of those locations are
within Google Cloud data

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:51.150
centers, where you're
doing compute today.

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:53.480
But we're also in other
satellite locations

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:56.780
to extend the reach
of the platform.

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:59.640
Interconnecting
all of those nodes

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.430
are Google controlled Fiber.

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:04.400
So unlike some other
competitors in the marketplace,

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.520
Cloud CDN interconnects
all edge nodes

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:09.360
with Google controlled Fiber.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:13.070
So when there is a cache
miss on the edge server,

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:18.440
you are routed your request back
to the Google origin location,

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:20.690
across Google controlled Fiber.

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:25.250
And this is a key
differentiator of Cloud CDN.

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:28.610
Now in our next segment that
we're going to discuss today,

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:31.970
I want to highlight some recent
releases of the Cloud CDN

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:33.260
platform.

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:36.170
Specifically, there are
kind of two areas of focus

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:39.290
that we really want to
segment content through.

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:41.023
There's performance
characteristics,

00:05:41.023 --> 00:05:42.440
and there are
feature enhancements

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:44.900
that we've recently released.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:48.770
The first feature we want
to talk about is TLS 1.3.

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:51.980
This was recently
released in April

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:54.740
of this year, where the
entire fleet has been

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:57.530
upgraded to support TLS 1.3.

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:02.940
One of the main benefits
of TLS 1.3 is it is faster.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:06.614
It's about 40%
faster over TLS 1.2.

00:06:06.614 --> 00:06:12.650
And that really translates
itself in time to first byte.

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:14.510
By reducing the
number of round trips

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:16.430
a client needs to
make with the server

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:19.520
in order to establish an SSL
connection, the faster we

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:22.580
can start delivering the
payload, the content,

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:24.890
that the end user
really cares about.

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:28.340
One of the other enhancements
that's made to TLS 1.3

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:32.360
is that it's removed some of
the obsolete insecure ciphers

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:38.880
that have been found in previous
versions of TLS 1.1 and 1.2.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:42.780
Approximately 80%
plus of end users

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:45.850
can support TLS 1.3 today.

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:48.180
So in order to take
advantage of this,

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:50.960
there's nothing that the
end user needs to do.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:53.370
Their browsers
already support it.

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:57.360
Cloud CDN just makes this
available as a protocol

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:02.010
option for the end user to
communicate with the Cloud CDN

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:04.000
server.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:07.390
The next feature we want to
talk about is Custom Origins.

00:07:07.390 --> 00:07:09.730
Prior to the release
of Custom Origins,

00:07:09.730 --> 00:07:14.560
the only way for content to
be fulfilled on Cloud CDN

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:18.220
is through infrastructure,
through content

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:22.270
that's actually hosted and
domiciled on Google Cloud.

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:24.220
Whether that be a
storage instance,

00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:26.380
or a compute instance.

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:29.020
With the introduction
of Custom Origins,

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:32.260
you now can attach
third party origins

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:35.770
that are not part of
your Google Cloud tenant,

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:40.540
in order to have that be
delivered by Cloud CDN.

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:42.430
So this now gives
you the ability

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:47.320
to extend the life of your
existing origin infrastructure

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:49.660
and still support Cloud CDN.

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:53.560
This also can be useful as
part of a migration strategy.

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:57.430
As you move content
into Google Cloud,

00:07:57.430 --> 00:08:00.190
you can use Cloud
CDN as a bridge

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:03.070
to kind of up your
origin infrastructure,

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:05.920
migrate your infrastructure
into Google Cloud,

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:08.410
and then change the
pointer of the backend.

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:13.180
So this becomes a huge
enabler for not only

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:18.170
Cloud CDN, but also as part of
a content migration strategy.

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:21.190
The other thing to kind of point
out here as a differentiator

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:23.530
is with the Custom Origins.

00:08:23.530 --> 00:08:28.000
We support not only
HTTP, HTTPS 1.1,

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.940
but we also support HTTP/2.

00:08:30.940 --> 00:08:35.260
So while the end user
client to the Cloud CDN Edge

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:40.630
can support HTTP/2, many other
content delivery providers

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:44.980
downgrade the protocol from the
edge talking back to an origin.

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:47.440
With Custom Origins,
you have the ability

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:51.340
to maintain the HTTP
protocol from edge

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:52.962
all the way to the origin.

00:08:52.962 --> 00:08:54.670
So you can take
advantage of head of line

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:58.630
blocking and multiplexing
capabilities of the HTTP/2

00:08:58.630 --> 00:09:01.320
protocol.

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:04.560
From an integration perspective,
something to be cognizant of

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:07.360
is with these third
party origins,

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:10.120
they still must be
publicly available.

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:14.610
So you can't use an RFC 1918
private IP address for this.

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:18.480
It still must be a publicly
available IP address.

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:21.930
Subsequently, you also--
that web server also

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:24.940
needs to be an HTTP
oriented web server,

00:09:24.940 --> 00:09:30.100
whether it's HTTP or HTTPS in
terms of its protocol exposure.

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:36.058
So you can't use any UDP
style protocol there.

00:09:36.058 --> 00:09:37.600
The next feature I
want to highlight,

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:41.230
that was recently enhanced
to the Cloud CDN platform,

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:43.430
is along the lines
of access control.

00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:47.860
Now prior to the release
of signed cookies,

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:50.680
we always could've support
query strings as a way

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:57.970
to mitigate content access,
and to help prevent the hyper

00:09:57.970 --> 00:10:00.310
distribution of content.

00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:03.700
We've now introduced the
ability to support sign cookies.

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:06.760
So not only can you use query
strings or sign cookies,

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:09.040
you can actually
use them together

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:12.850
as a way to provide more
comprehensive content

00:10:12.850 --> 00:10:14.710
protection strategy.

00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:16.570
Generally speaking
though, if you

00:10:16.570 --> 00:10:20.530
think of how the Access
Control works today,

00:10:20.530 --> 00:10:24.060
there are three core
elements to be cognizant of.

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:26.200
There's epic time,
this is the time

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:29.560
of the expiration
of the URI asset.

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:32.950
There's the key, by default
Google can support up

00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:35.480
to three keys simultaneously.

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:39.340
And you can rotate those keys
at will through your Cloud CDN

00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:40.660
configuration.

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:43.180
And lastly, there's
the hashed value

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:49.930
that you use to create that
secure token value based

00:10:49.930 --> 00:10:54.040
on the key and the URI path
that you want to incorporate

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:56.290
as part of the security check.

00:10:56.290 --> 00:11:00.070
All three things need to be
present as part of your Access

00:11:00.070 --> 00:11:05.250
Control to prevent the hyper
distribution of the URL.

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:08.610
Now one of the things that's
unique about Cloud CDN

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:11.940
is that there are other
ancillary functions

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:16.020
within Google Cloud that
actively support the Cloud CDN

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:17.260
service.

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:20.580
So if you think about how
Cloud CDN comes to life,

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:23.580
it leans on these
other capabilities

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:27.390
of the wider portfolio
to enhance the offering.

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:29.820
In this portion
of our discussion,

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:33.210
I want to kind of highlight a
couple of those new features

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:35.430
that were recently
released that provide

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:40.455
additional functionality to
the Cloud CDN user experience.

00:11:40.455 --> 00:11:42.080
The first feature we
want to talk about

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:44.120
is redirects and rewrites.

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:48.980
And this particular function
is an extension of the Google

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:51.290
Global Load Balancer.

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:56.120
Today we now have the ability
to perform redirects and URL

00:11:56.120 --> 00:11:57.530
modifications.

00:11:57.530 --> 00:12:01.250
Prior to this, you had to do
any sort of rewrites or URL

00:12:01.250 --> 00:12:04.940
modifications on your
origin infrastructure.

00:12:04.940 --> 00:12:09.500
With this new release, the
Google Global Load Balancer

00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:13.950
has the ability to
dynamically do the rewrites,

00:12:13.950 --> 00:12:15.440
or redirections.

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:18.260
We see this quite often
in terms of use cases

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:22.250
for things like HTTP
to HTTPS modifications.

00:12:22.250 --> 00:12:25.880
We also see this as part of a
URL shortener strategy, where

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:30.290
you might have a short
friendly URI path for marketing

00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:32.880
purposes, but behind
the scenes you

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:36.080
want to explode the
URL to actually point

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:41.500
to the actual
origin destination.

00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:44.680
The next feature we want to
talk about as a highlight

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:47.980
and extension to Cloud
CDN is on logging.

00:12:47.980 --> 00:12:50.440
More specifically,
there are two edge cases

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:52.420
that we want to highlight here.

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:56.050
Number one is the
edge cache status.

00:12:56.050 --> 00:12:59.080
Prior to this release,
the Cloud CDN platform

00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:03.550
never exposed the behavior
of the edge cache.

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:05.800
Whether or not we
served it from cache,

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:08.140
or we had to fetch
that from the origin.

00:13:08.140 --> 00:13:12.460
With this latest enhancement,
we now expose this information

00:13:12.460 --> 00:13:14.860
to you in the log line.

00:13:14.860 --> 00:13:17.350
So you now have the ability
to see whether or not

00:13:17.350 --> 00:13:22.960
the actual edge cache served it
from its own stores, or disk,

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:25.670
or if we had to fetch
it from the origin.

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:28.450
This can be important in
helping you figure out

00:13:28.450 --> 00:13:32.150
your optimized content
offload strategy.

00:13:32.150 --> 00:13:34.690
And so you can set
alerts, you can

00:13:34.690 --> 00:13:38.830
set monitoring against
these data attributes

00:13:38.830 --> 00:13:42.910
in the logline to help
improve your offload rates.

00:13:42.910 --> 00:13:44.680
The other enhancement
that was recently

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:49.600
released inside the
loglines is the IATA code

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:55.000
of the Google Cloud CDN edge
server that did the delivery.

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:59.280
The IATA code is a
three character code

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:01.570
that sometimes is
referred to as the airport

00:14:01.570 --> 00:14:07.000
code of where that Google
Cloud CDN edge server actually

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:08.140
resides.

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:13.330
So the idea behind this is, you
have some visibility as to how

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:17.260
Google is actually delivering
your content to end users.

00:14:17.260 --> 00:14:21.040
Now mind you, this is not
the actual destination

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:23.870
of where the end
user is located.

00:14:23.870 --> 00:14:25.660
This is merely the
edge server that

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:31.510
was used to fulfill that
specific edge request.

00:14:31.510 --> 00:14:35.920
The next feature I want to
highlight here is Cloud Armor.

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:40.465
We've recently now enabled Cloud
Armor to work with the Cloud

00:14:40.465 --> 00:14:43.480
CDN origin infrastructure.

00:14:43.480 --> 00:14:46.270
For those of you that are not
familiar with Cloud Armor,

00:14:46.270 --> 00:14:50.170
Cloud Armor is Google's
inline security offering

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:53.740
for WAF and network
layer protection.

00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:57.250
And this is designed to
help protect origin compute

00:14:57.250 --> 00:14:58.360
resources.

00:14:58.360 --> 00:15:01.030
Specifically,
within Cloud Armor,

00:15:01.030 --> 00:15:04.270
it supports OWASP
ModSecurity rules,

00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:07.600
but it also supports IP
white listing/ blacklisting,

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:11.440
as well as country level IP
whitelisting/ blacklisting.

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:14.800
The benefit of using both
Cloud Armor and the Cloud CDN

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:18.430
platform together, is it
gives you fantastic volumetric

00:15:18.430 --> 00:15:20.100
protection.

00:15:20.100 --> 00:15:24.360
But it also provides
presentation layer protection,

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:28.350
such that you can now
protect origin workloads

00:15:28.350 --> 00:15:33.420
and deflect requests that have
been detected by the security

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:38.220
rules, or your IP or country
list blocking schema,

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:41.520
so that your origins don't need
to deal with those requests

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:46.410
that we know are basically
of malicious intent.

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:50.160
The other benefit of working
with Cloud Armor and the Cloud

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:55.800
CDN platform together is that
the messaging for Cloud Armor

00:15:55.800 --> 00:16:00.840
is directly embedded within
Google Load Balancer loglines.

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:04.530
So with both of these
solutions in place,

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:07.080
you'll have a single
source of truth

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:09.660
where you have
visibility in terms

00:16:09.660 --> 00:16:11.310
of what is allowed or denied.

00:16:11.310 --> 00:16:14.080
And if we denied or
allowed a request,

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:18.510
you can see the policy rule from
Cloud Armor that specifically

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:23.620
was being enforced in order to
effect that specific logline

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:24.120
request.

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:30.850
Now we'd like to turn a little
bit into the second half

00:16:30.850 --> 00:16:32.090
of the year.

00:16:32.090 --> 00:16:36.268
What are we intending to bring
to market within Cloud CDN?

00:16:36.268 --> 00:16:37.810
The first feature
I want to highlight

00:16:37.810 --> 00:16:40.090
is Flexible Cache Control.

00:16:40.090 --> 00:16:41.920
Within Flexible
Cache Control, there

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:46.570
are three core capabilities
that fall under this umbrella.

00:16:46.570 --> 00:16:50.500
There is Cache Control,
there's Header Manipulation,

00:16:50.500 --> 00:16:53.240
and there's Error Handling.

00:16:53.240 --> 00:16:57.560
As we talk about Cache Control
before the Flexible Cache

00:16:57.560 --> 00:17:01.550
Control release, Cache
Control header values

00:17:01.550 --> 00:17:05.089
need to be dictated by
your origin web server.

00:17:05.089 --> 00:17:06.650
And many customers
have complained

00:17:06.650 --> 00:17:08.390
that that was not
how they wanted

00:17:08.390 --> 00:17:12.170
to control the cache setup.

00:17:12.170 --> 00:17:15.250
With the Flexible
Cache Control release,

00:17:15.250 --> 00:17:18.829
the Cloud CDN platform
now will have the ability

00:17:18.829 --> 00:17:24.450
to apply cache control rules
on CDN platform itself.

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:27.530
This will help improve
the cache offload rates

00:17:27.530 --> 00:17:31.010
by you no longer having to
manipulate your origin web

00:17:31.010 --> 00:17:31.610
server.

00:17:31.610 --> 00:17:34.430
But it also simplifies
the workflow process

00:17:34.430 --> 00:17:38.060
where the CDN automatically
will apply the rules,

00:17:38.060 --> 00:17:39.590
rather than you
having to be more

00:17:39.590 --> 00:17:44.390
specific about your work and
path and header manipulation

00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:48.020
within your web
server configuration.

00:17:48.020 --> 00:17:50.790
Specifically from an
integration standpoint,

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:54.690
we will have three
main setting options.

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:58.850
So we will still maintain
origin cache control values,

00:17:58.850 --> 00:18:01.070
and that still will
be available to you.

00:18:01.070 --> 00:18:03.890
But we're going to add
two new capabilities

00:18:03.890 --> 00:18:09.200
for being able to serve cached
objects, for static objects,

00:18:09.200 --> 00:18:13.340
as well as the ability to
just simply cache everything.

00:18:13.340 --> 00:18:16.640
Where in this case, we will
ignore whatever decoration

00:18:16.640 --> 00:18:20.700
the origin is supplying in terms
of cache control header values,

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:23.120
whether it's private,
public, et cetera,

00:18:23.120 --> 00:18:27.080
and we'll just apply our
own TTL setting values based

00:18:27.080 --> 00:18:29.030
on your configuration set.

00:18:29.030 --> 00:18:33.620
In the case where you're
using cached static objects

00:18:33.620 --> 00:18:36.790
mode of operation,
what we will do

00:18:36.790 --> 00:18:39.670
is we will apply the
cache control values

00:18:39.670 --> 00:18:42.640
that you dictate in
your CDN configuration

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:44.650
based on specific mind types.

00:18:44.650 --> 00:18:48.070
And those mine types are
listed here on our slide.

00:18:48.070 --> 00:18:51.940
The second component
we want to talk about

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:54.760
within Flexible Cache Control
is Header Manipulation.

00:18:54.760 --> 00:18:56.890
And within Header
Manipulation, there

00:18:56.890 --> 00:18:58.540
are a couple of
different use cases

00:18:58.540 --> 00:19:01.330
that will be addressed
with this release.

00:19:01.330 --> 00:19:04.900
The first one is the ability to
add downstream response headers

00:19:04.900 --> 00:19:06.770
to the end user client.

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:09.760
This is helpful for things
like adding course header

00:19:09.760 --> 00:19:14.050
values, which can be important
for or streamed objects,

00:19:14.050 --> 00:19:17.590
or JavaScript objects, that
require additional helper

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:22.270
module, helper directives to
allow the browser to leverage

00:19:22.270 --> 00:19:25.980
the particular object
that is being delivered.

00:19:25.980 --> 00:19:30.090
The next capability within
the Header Manipulation bucket

00:19:30.090 --> 00:19:34.350
is the ability to bypass a
cache on a header request.

00:19:34.350 --> 00:19:36.570
And this can be
helpful to distinguish

00:19:36.570 --> 00:19:39.630
between an authorized
and an unauthorized user.

00:19:39.630 --> 00:19:41.370
Or it could be used
to determine things

00:19:41.370 --> 00:19:43.750
like a logged in or
a logged in user.

00:19:43.750 --> 00:19:47.850
So for example, you might
want to have a cached page

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:51.360
experience for
unauthenticated users.

00:19:51.360 --> 00:19:54.660
In the case where you have a
more personalized experience

00:19:54.660 --> 00:19:56.850
and the user actually
is authenticated,

00:19:56.850 --> 00:19:59.100
you might want to
bypass the cache

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:01.980
and go directly to the
origin for a more customized

00:20:01.980 --> 00:20:02.640
experience.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:07.710
Deliver it up from
the origin itself.

00:20:07.710 --> 00:20:10.610
The third use case under
the Header Manipulation

00:20:10.610 --> 00:20:13.980
is the ability to restrict the
methods that are being used.

00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:19.160
So by default, Google is going
to allow all different method

00:20:19.160 --> 00:20:23.990
types to get funneled into
your origin web server.

00:20:23.990 --> 00:20:28.010
With this new capability out
of the Flexible Cache Control,

00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:31.580
we now have the ability to
be more prescriptive in terms

00:20:31.580 --> 00:20:35.300
of what feature
method types that we

00:20:35.300 --> 00:20:38.630
want to allow into the origin.

00:20:38.630 --> 00:20:42.860
The benefit here is that this
will allow you to tighten up

00:20:42.860 --> 00:20:44.190
your security policy.

00:20:44.190 --> 00:20:48.350
So for example, if your website
doesn't accept post requests,

00:20:48.350 --> 00:20:53.630
then we can specifically deny
post requests on the CDN Edge,

00:20:53.630 --> 00:20:56.270
rather than funneling those
requests all the way back

00:20:56.270 --> 00:20:58.880
to the origin where
potentially it

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:03.900
could be ripe for
vulnerability or exploitation.

00:21:03.900 --> 00:21:06.080
The other part of this,
when we talk about things

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:07.970
like adding course
headers, it helps

00:21:07.970 --> 00:21:10.490
enrich the client
requests experience

00:21:10.490 --> 00:21:14.810
so that we can take advantage
of capabilities that

00:21:14.810 --> 00:21:16.790
are native inside the browser.

00:21:16.790 --> 00:21:20.670
Such as cores capabilities.

00:21:20.670 --> 00:21:23.050
The third extension of
the Flexible Cache Control

00:21:23.050 --> 00:21:24.540
is error handling.

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:28.440
Prior to the release of Flexible
Cache Control and the error

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:31.650
handling module, the
Cloud CDN platform

00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:34.020
would pass through
whatever error

00:21:34.020 --> 00:21:37.110
that was handed to
it from the origin.

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:39.030
And this kind of
manifested itself

00:21:39.030 --> 00:21:42.270
in reduced site availability.

00:21:42.270 --> 00:21:44.390
We're making
modifications to the way

00:21:44.390 --> 00:21:49.580
that Cloud CDN behaves to
enhance your site availability.

00:21:49.580 --> 00:21:53.220
The fist extension of that
is with negative caching.

00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:57.500
So specifically, if you
advertise certain HTTP status

00:21:57.500 --> 00:22:00.110
codes from your origin,
we have the ability

00:22:00.110 --> 00:22:05.240
to actually cache those requests
to help buffer your origin

00:22:05.240 --> 00:22:08.860
from these responses.

00:22:08.860 --> 00:22:11.320
The second extension
specifically

00:22:11.320 --> 00:22:15.680
around Error Handling, is the
ability to serve stale content.

00:22:15.680 --> 00:22:18.220
So by default, when
an end user request

00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:20.980
is made to the Cloud
CDN Edge, we're

00:22:20.980 --> 00:22:23.500
going to make a
determination if we have

00:22:23.500 --> 00:22:24.940
that content we'll serve it.

00:22:24.940 --> 00:22:28.210
If we don't have that content,
but we have the object sitting

00:22:28.210 --> 00:22:30.940
in cache, we're going to
consult with the origin

00:22:30.940 --> 00:22:34.005
for revalidation of that object.

00:22:34.005 --> 00:22:35.380
Of whether or not
we can continue

00:22:35.380 --> 00:22:37.900
to serve that out of our cache.

00:22:37.900 --> 00:22:41.530
In the event that the
origin isn't available,

00:22:41.530 --> 00:22:45.520
we would in the past serve
up an error response.

00:22:45.520 --> 00:22:49.330
With this new capability,
we now have the ability

00:22:49.330 --> 00:22:52.270
to serve that stale content
rather than serving up

00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:53.930
the error page.

00:22:53.930 --> 00:22:57.430
So this is going to, again,
help increase your website

00:22:57.430 --> 00:22:58.360
availability.

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:03.630
But it also helps buffer your
origin at times of high load.

00:23:03.630 --> 00:23:09.060
From an integration perspective,
some helpful tips to share

00:23:09.060 --> 00:23:09.850
with you.

00:23:09.850 --> 00:23:13.500
So first off, by default
Google will be turning

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:15.930
on serving of stale content.

00:23:15.930 --> 00:23:21.180
By default. The TTL
default value is one day.

00:23:21.180 --> 00:23:26.070
So in the event where we have
an object in our edge cache

00:23:26.070 --> 00:23:28.890
and we can't revalidate
it with the origin,

00:23:28.890 --> 00:23:31.590
the default posture
will be to serve out

00:23:31.590 --> 00:23:36.290
stale, rather than serving
up an error message.

00:23:36.290 --> 00:23:38.720
Subsequently, the
negative caching

00:23:38.720 --> 00:23:43.020
will be an opt in feature, where
it will be available to you,

00:23:43.020 --> 00:23:47.300
but you must elect to configure
and turn that behavior

00:23:47.300 --> 00:23:50.150
on in your configuration set.

00:23:50.150 --> 00:23:56.630
That negative caching will
not be enabled by default.

00:23:56.630 --> 00:23:59.395
In our next level
of enhancements

00:23:59.395 --> 00:24:00.770
that I would like
to discuss with

00:24:00.770 --> 00:24:03.380
you is around
Platform Enhancements.

00:24:03.380 --> 00:24:05.330
And the first feature
we'll want to highlight

00:24:05.330 --> 00:24:07.820
is Resource Coalescing.

00:24:07.820 --> 00:24:10.790
With Resource Coalescing
this helps, particularly

00:24:10.790 --> 00:24:12.440
with things like
live streaming, when

00:24:12.440 --> 00:24:15.800
we have lots of requests all
looking for the same object.

00:24:15.800 --> 00:24:19.460
In the past each request
would be treated individually.

00:24:19.460 --> 00:24:21.830
With Resource
Coalescing, or sometimes

00:24:21.830 --> 00:24:25.620
called request collapsing,
we have the ability

00:24:25.620 --> 00:24:30.650
to fulfill that request
for multiple edge requests

00:24:30.650 --> 00:24:34.580
from a single request, and share
out that response to the edge

00:24:34.580 --> 00:24:38.510
server, rather than making a
lots of individual requests.

00:24:38.510 --> 00:24:42.020
This helps with content
offload from the origin,

00:24:42.020 --> 00:24:48.240
and also helps with content
scaling for high volume events.

00:24:48.240 --> 00:24:50.900
The next platform enhancement
that I want to talk to you

00:24:50.900 --> 00:24:53.280
about is TCP Enhancements.

00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:58.730
So as part of our recurring
theme of always improving

00:24:58.730 --> 00:25:01.730
our time to first byte,
and also improving

00:25:01.730 --> 00:25:03.980
our throughput
response rates, we

00:25:03.980 --> 00:25:06.590
are making
modifications to the way

00:25:06.590 --> 00:25:11.060
that Cloud CDN will interact
with the end user client.

00:25:11.060 --> 00:25:14.070
All of this is invisible
to the end user.

00:25:14.070 --> 00:25:17.030
All of this is invisible to
you, from a configuration

00:25:17.030 --> 00:25:17.780
standpoint.

00:25:17.780 --> 00:25:21.000
It just magically
will get better.

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:23.100
Specifically, on the
TCP Enhancements,

00:25:23.100 --> 00:25:26.020
we're making modifications
to our throughput,

00:25:26.020 --> 00:25:28.840
but we're also making
enhancements to our pacing.

00:25:28.840 --> 00:25:34.800
So today we're using pacing
that leverages BBR, for example.

00:25:34.800 --> 00:25:36.300
One of the things
that we're looking

00:25:36.300 --> 00:25:39.570
at adding into the platform
in the second semester

00:25:39.570 --> 00:25:42.870
is looking at adding
the BBR version two.

00:25:42.870 --> 00:25:48.610
Again, another advancement
coming from Google.

00:25:48.610 --> 00:25:52.810
The next item that I want to
talk to you about is HTTP/3.

00:25:52.810 --> 00:25:57.730
So today we support QUIC,
more specifically gQUIC.

00:25:57.730 --> 00:26:00.130
And this can be found
already embedded

00:26:00.130 --> 00:26:03.760
and it's already enabled on
Chrome and the latest Firefox

00:26:03.760 --> 00:26:05.800
browsers today.

00:26:05.800 --> 00:26:07.900
And this is now enabled
on your platform

00:26:07.900 --> 00:26:11.170
that you can enable and opt
in to, in your Cloud CDN

00:26:11.170 --> 00:26:12.820
configuration.

00:26:12.820 --> 00:26:14.560
But we're not stopping there.

00:26:14.560 --> 00:26:20.950
We are now going to be extending
the QUIC platform from gQUIC

00:26:20.950 --> 00:26:22.960
and we're transforming
that to iQUIC,

00:26:22.960 --> 00:26:27.480
which is now the new standard
for the QUIC protocol.

00:26:27.480 --> 00:26:30.640
The iQUIC is kind of
a precursor of sorts

00:26:30.640 --> 00:26:33.040
to the HTTP/3 protocol.

00:26:33.040 --> 00:26:38.590
And as we start to rollout
iQUIC and the HTTP/3 protocol

00:26:38.590 --> 00:26:42.700
to the entire Cloud CDN
fleet, Cloud CDN customers

00:26:42.700 --> 00:26:45.820
will automatically
have the ability

00:26:45.820 --> 00:26:48.430
to leverage this new protocol.

00:26:48.430 --> 00:26:51.670
The benefit of using
the HTTP/3 protocol

00:26:51.670 --> 00:26:55.090
is going to improve not only
your time to first byte,

00:26:55.090 --> 00:26:58.150
but also improve the client
performance and stability

00:26:58.150 --> 00:26:59.830
for end user
clients, particularly

00:26:59.830 --> 00:27:02.650
on unstable networks.

00:27:02.650 --> 00:27:05.160
So in summary, the
Cloud CDN platform

00:27:05.160 --> 00:27:09.040
is making a lot of
investment in our platform,

00:27:09.040 --> 00:27:11.430
not only in terms of enhancing
and adding new features

00:27:11.430 --> 00:27:14.650
and functionality to
the Cloud CDN platform,

00:27:14.650 --> 00:27:17.910
but we're also looking
at extending our industry

00:27:17.910 --> 00:27:19.950
leading performance
characteristics

00:27:19.950 --> 00:27:22.570
and capabilities.

00:27:22.570 --> 00:27:25.870
Likewise, we're also making
additional investments

00:27:25.870 --> 00:27:29.150
in Cloud CDN to revamp
the user experience.

00:27:29.150 --> 00:27:32.980
So it's going to become
easier and a more enriching

00:27:32.980 --> 00:27:37.900
environment for you to use
Cloud CDN without having to go

00:27:37.900 --> 00:27:41.140
to multiple different screens.

00:27:41.140 --> 00:27:42.640
And with that, I
want to thank you

00:27:42.640 --> 00:27:45.450
for attending my
presentation today,

00:27:45.450 --> 00:27:49.050
and I welcome to see you
on a future presentation.

